WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.440
- If Elon Musk is in the news

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.090
it's usually for one of three reasons:

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.970
he's announcing a Tesla product
that will never materialize,

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.370
he's revealing the
existence of another child,

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:11.190
or he's pretending to buy a
company and then backing out.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.290
Well, Tesla doesn't have any new products,

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.630
he's up to 10 kids that we know of,

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.750
so Elon Musk now wants to back out

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:20.580
of his purchase of Twitter.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.220
Although some headlines said,
"Musk cancels Twitter deal,"

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:24.660
that's not quite accurate.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.520
Musk has informed Twitter that he believes

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.890
that the company is in breach
of the acquisition agreement

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.230
and that he therefore will no
longer perform the contract

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.730
by purchasing the company.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:34.920
Twitter, on the other
hand, says that it will sue

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.510
to force Musk to buy the company

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.250
at the original sales price.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.170
So this raises all sorts of questions.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.420
Can Elon get out of the
deal because of bots?

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.060
Can Musk pay $1 billion
to cancel the contract?

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:48.660
And if Twitter sues, can a
judge force Musk to go through

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.003
with this multi-billion dollar deal?

00:00:51.003 --> 00:00:53.670
(upbeat music)

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.930
So how did Elon Musk find
himself in this situation?

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:01.740
Well, Musk is an
avid-slash-obsessed Twitter user.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.680
In early 2022, Musk
initiated conversations

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.560
with Twitter bigwigs and
started buying Twitter stock.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.950
In February and March, Musk
accumulated over 9% ownership

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:11.940
of Twitter's common shares

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.440
without formally disclosing his interest

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.850
in a potential purchase, which
was a violation of SEC rules.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:19.950
The SEC mandates that anyone who acquires

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:22.800
more than 5% of the
company's common shares

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.650
has to disclose those holdings
within 10 calendar days.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:30.420
Musk finally signed his filing
21 days after March 14th,

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.120
the date his stake surpassed 5%.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.640
Now after accepting and
then rejecting a seat

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.870
on Twitter's board of directors,

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:41.070
Elon Musk made an offer to
purchase Twitter for $44 billion.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.200
After a bunch of back and
forth, Twitter agreed to sell

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.430
if Musk could come up with the financing,

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:48.660
eventually settling on a
purchase price at $54.20 a share,

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.170
which is entirely possible
to be a 4/20 joke.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:53.310
Now for reasons I won't go into,

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:54.900
Musk styled the deal as a merger

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.550
with a specially created company,

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:58.140
so if you hear the term merger agreement

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.450
you can just think of purchase agreement.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.790
And on April 25th,
Twitter and Musk announced

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:03.630
a joint agreement.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.257
Quote, "Free speech is the bedrock

00:02:05.257 --> 00:02:06.213
"of a functioning democracy,

00:02:06.213 --> 00:02:08.707
"and Twitter is the digital town square

00:02:08.707 --> 00:02:11.760
"where matters vital to the
future of humanity are debated."

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.557
Musk also said, quote, "I also
want to make Twitter better

00:02:14.557 --> 00:02:16.837
"than ever by enhancing the
product with new features,

00:02:16.837 --> 00:02:19.567
"making the algorithms open
source to increase trust,

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:22.920
"defeating the spam bots, and
authenticating all humans."

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.710
But on May 15th, Musk
started trying to backtrack

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:26.580
on the deal.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.800
He tweeted that he was
putting the deal on hold

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.890
because he believed that more
than 5% of Twitter's user base

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:33.180
was made up of bots.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.580
A bot in this context
means an automated account

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.380
that isn't linked to a real person.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.690
Now Twitter gave Musk more
information about bots,

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:42.030
but on July 8th, Musk told the SEC

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.880
that he was exercising his
right to terminate the contract.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.860
Twitter fired back that it will sue Musk

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:48.510
to have him buy the company.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.430
So can Musk end the contract,

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:53.100
or can Twitter sue him for damages?

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.290
Well, everything hinges on
the contractual language

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.870
that Musk says gives him the
right to terminate the deal.

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.450
So let's look at the letter
that Musk sent to the SEC

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:01.680
claiming to back out of the deal,

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.420
and let's look at the contract provisions

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:05.550
to see what rights are afforded.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:08.370
Now mergers and acquisitions
transactions often involve

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.200
the disclosure or
transfer of personal data

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.180
from a seller to a purchaser.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.250
This may include all data
about the target company

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.170
and its assets like data about employees,

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:18.450
customers, users, and contractors.

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.040
Now one point of clarification here,

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:21.690
this is not due diligence.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.340
Due diligence is something you do

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:27.720
before you enter into a merger
or acquisition agreement.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.990
But in this case Musk didn't
do very much due diligence.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:34.080
He signed the merger agreement
without having access

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.140
to much inside information from Twitter.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.600
Here Musk says that he is
terminating the contract

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.280
because Twitter violated its obligations

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.800
to provide him with access to information

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:45.450
that is required under the contract.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.430
But crucially, this is not information

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:48.990
for due diligence purposes.

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:50.940
The time for due diligence is long over.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.110
It was over the second

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.270
that Elon Musk signed
the merger agreement.

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:57.060
But at any rate, section
6.4 of the merger contract

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.850
is entitled Access to
Information and Confidentiality.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.680
This is the clause that
Musk says Twitter breached,

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.697
which states, quote,
"Upon reasonable notice,

00:04:03.697 --> 00:04:06.667
"the company shall and shall
cause each of its subsidiaries

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:08.767
"to afford to the
representatives of parent's

00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:11.347
"reasonable access at parent's
sole costs and expense

00:04:11.347 --> 00:04:14.227
"in a manner not disruptive
in any material respect

00:04:14.227 --> 00:04:16.177
"to the operations of the business

00:04:16.177 --> 00:04:17.827
"of the company and its subsidiaries

00:04:17.827 --> 00:04:19.747
"to the properties, books,
and records of the company

00:04:19.747 --> 00:04:20.707
"and its subsidiaries,

00:04:20.707 --> 00:04:22.657
"and shall cause each of its subsidiaries

00:04:22.657 --> 00:04:24.697
"to furnish promptly two representatives

00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:26.857
"all information concerning
the business, properties,

00:04:26.857 --> 00:04:29.677
"and personnel of the
company and its subsidiaries

00:04:29.677 --> 00:04:32.497
"as may reasonably be requested
in writing, in each case,

00:04:32.497 --> 00:04:33.997
"for any reasonable business purpose

00:04:33.997 --> 00:04:36.307
"related to the consummation
of the transactions

00:04:36.307 --> 00:04:38.220
"contemplated by this agreement."

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:39.247
Now that is lawyer speak for,

00:04:39.247 --> 00:04:41.850
"Twitter must provide Musk
with company information."

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:43.320
And there are all sorts of caveats.

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.420
It must be related to a business purpose.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:48.030
Query whether Musk's requests are related

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.460
to a business purpose or
to screw over the business,

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:51.420
it's hard to say.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.587
But Musk's legal team
has said that, quote,

00:04:53.587 --> 00:04:55.987
"Twitter has not complied with
its contractual obligations

00:04:55.987 --> 00:04:57.510
"under section 6.4."

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:58.987
Quote, "For nearly two months,

00:04:58.987 --> 00:05:01.807
"Mr. Musk has sought the
data information necessary

00:05:01.807 --> 00:05:04.687
"to make an independent assessment
of the prevalence of fake

00:05:04.687 --> 00:05:06.967
"or spam accounts on Twitter's platform.

00:05:06.967 --> 00:05:09.187
"This information is fundamental
to Twitter's business

00:05:09.187 --> 00:05:11.317
"and financial performance
and is necessary

00:05:11.317 --> 00:05:13.087
"to consummate the
transactions contemplated

00:05:13.087 --> 00:05:15.397
"by the merger agreement
because it is needed to ensure

00:05:15.397 --> 00:05:17.917
"Twitter's satisfaction of
the conditions to closing,

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:20.827
"to facilitate Mr. Musk's
financing and financial planning

00:05:20.827 --> 00:05:21.847
"for the transaction,

00:05:21.847 --> 00:05:25.560
"and to engage in transaction
planning for the business."

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.270
So according to Musk's lawyers,

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.250
Twitter breached its
contractual obligations

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:30.330
to close the deal.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.180
The centerpiece of the letter
is that Twitter misrepresented

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.640
the number of bots and refused
to give Musk information

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:39.390
that would identify the true
number of bots on the platform.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:41.580
Though the lawyers
couched it very carefully.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:45.157
Quote, "Preliminary analysis
by Mr. Musk's advisors

00:05:45.157 --> 00:05:47.497
"of the information
provided by Twitter to date

00:05:47.497 --> 00:05:49.987
"causes Mr. Musk to strongly believe

00:05:49.987 --> 00:05:51.757
"that the proportion of
false and spam accounts

00:05:51.757 --> 00:05:54.727
"included in the reported MDAU count

00:05:54.727 --> 00:05:57.270
"is wildly higher than 5%."

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:00.240
Now that is not a factual
claim about anything

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:03.180
except what Elon Musk personally believes,

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.830
which is a very sneaky way

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.530
of avoiding an actual factual claim

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:10.860
that could subject the lawyers
to a little bit more scrutiny

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:14.790
and Musk in general to more liability.

00:06:14.790 --> 00:06:16.500
But let's hear about it
from the man himself.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:17.910
Elon went on a podcast to explain

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:19.080
that Twitter has so many bots

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:20.517
that it makes Twitter a
totally different company

00:06:20.517 --> 00:06:22.560
than the one he thought he was buying.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:23.550
Quote-
- You know,

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:27.690
if they in fact have been firstly claiming

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:30.420
less than 5% of fake or spam accounts,

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.820
but in fact it is four or
five times that number,

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:36.273
or perhaps 10 times that
number, this is a big deal.

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:39.520
It's sort of like if you said, "Okay,

00:06:40.717 --> 00:06:42.210
"I agree to buy your house."

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:45.233
You say the house has
less than 5% termites.

00:06:45.233 --> 00:06:47.250
That's an acceptable number.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:52.110
But if it turns out it is 90%
termites, that's not okay.

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.510
You know, it's not the same house.

00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:56.820
- Now if Twitter is 90% bots,

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.370
then Elon Musk might be correct

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:01.440
for reasons that we'll
discuss in a moment.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.890
But is Twitter 90% bots? Probably not.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:06.840
But let's talk about
Twitter's obligations.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:08.760
Twitter responded to Musk
by giving him information

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:11.160
about how it estimates the
number of fake accounts.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:13.740
There's a long Twitter
thread by the Twitter CEO

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.780
that explains where the
calculations came from.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:18.067
He said the 5% estimate
is based on, quote,

00:07:18.067 --> 00:07:20.077
"Multiple human reviews in replicate

00:07:20.077 --> 00:07:23.197
"of thousands of accounts
that are sampled at random

00:07:23.197 --> 00:07:27.540
"consistently over time from
accounts we count as MDAUs."

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:29.460
Now Twitter claims that
it's human review process

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.340
uses both public and private
data like IP addresses,

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:36.000
geolocation, and other factors
to make a determination

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.520
about whether an account
belongs to a real person.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:40.470
Musk infamously responded to this thread

00:07:40.470 --> 00:07:41.910
with a simple poop emoji

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:46.170
because that is the level of
discourse that we face here.

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.330
But Twitter eventually
caved to Musk's demand

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:50.430
for more information by
giving him information

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:53.580
about every single tweet
in the Twitter universe.

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:56.400
This has been referred
to as the Firehose API,

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:59.070
a stream of every tweet that's been sent.

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:01.650
This dataset includes
records of user tweets,

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:04.950
the devices those users tweeted from,

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:06.630
and real time API data.

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:08.760
And despite receiving this information,

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:10.260
Musk says that he couldn't perform

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:11.520
the analysis he wanted to,

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:14.190
which may not be Twitter's problem,

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:16.770
but his lawyers identified
five more categories of stuff

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:18.990
that they wanted related to user accounts.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:21.960
But the bottom line here is
that Twitter probably gave Musk

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:25.590
all the data he could
reasonably be entitled to,

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.830
and the contract probably
doesn't require more of that.

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:32.100
But there is another wrinkle
here that deserves discussion

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:35.130
because even if Twitter
underestimated the number of bots

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:38.220
on the platform, Musk still
doesn't have the legal right

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:41.850
to cancel the agreement because
not all misrepresentations

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:43.590
give Musk the right to back out.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:45.120
Now in a normal contract

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.600
a material misrepresentation
could absolutely give rise

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:51.450
to canceling or terminating a contract,

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:53.970
but this is a different kind of contract

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:55.290
because people don't like to enter

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:56.610
into these merger agreements

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.130
and then have the
possibility of backing out.

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.710
And this contract would only be breached

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:03.607
if the misrepresentations had a, quote,

00:09:03.607 --> 00:09:05.880
"Material adverse effect."

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:08.580
Now the phrase material
adverse effect is a term of art

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:10.590
used in mergers and acquisitions law.

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:11.943
It's usually defined in the contract

00:09:11.943 --> 00:09:13.680
in a very long paragraph.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:15.810
And here the language has been interpreted

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:17.520
several times by Delaware Courts.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:20.130
We'll talk more about
why Delaware later on,

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:22.650
because that's the whole
thing in and of itself.

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:24.630
But in Delaware there is a high standard

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:26.760
for showing a material adverse effect.

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:29.610
The burden of proof is
usually on the buyer.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:32.010
Usually material adverse effect is invoked

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:33.510
when the target company starts having

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:34.800
significant performance problems.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:37.560
But even if a company's
performance is in decline

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:40.410
and it's dramatic, Delaware
Courts generally say

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:42.780
that the time period needs
to be measured in years,

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:44.310
not just a few bad months.

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:46.980
And as far as I can tell,
Delaware Courts had never found

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:48.570
a misrepresentation to give rise

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:50.070
to a material adverse effect

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:54.720
until 2018 in the case called
Akorn v. Fresenius Kabi AG.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:57.390
The Delaware Courts summarized
the buyer's burden of proof.

00:09:57.390 --> 00:09:59.617
Quote, "A buyer faces a heavy burden

00:09:59.617 --> 00:10:02.527
"when it attempts to invoke a
material adverse effect clause

00:10:02.527 --> 00:10:04.897
"in order to avoid its
obligations to close.

00:10:04.897 --> 00:10:07.777
"A short-term hiccup in
earnings should not suffice;

00:10:07.777 --> 00:10:09.847
"rather than material adverse
effects should be material

00:10:09.847 --> 00:10:12.007
"when viewed from the
longer-term perspective

00:10:12.007 --> 00:10:13.747
"of a reasonable acquirer.

00:10:13.747 --> 00:10:16.177
"In the absence of
evidence to the contrary,

00:10:16.177 --> 00:10:17.947
"a corporate acquirer may be assumed

00:10:17.947 --> 00:10:18.967
"to be purchasing the target

00:10:18.967 --> 00:10:21.127
"as part of a long-term strategy.

00:10:21.127 --> 00:10:22.507
"The important consideration therefore

00:10:22.507 --> 00:10:24.727
"is whether there has
been an adverse change

00:10:24.727 --> 00:10:26.887
"in the target's business
that is consequential

00:10:26.887 --> 00:10:29.257
"to the company's long-term earnings power

00:10:29.257 --> 00:10:31.687
"over a commercially reasonable period,

00:10:31.687 --> 00:10:33.757
"which one would expect
to be measured in years

00:10:33.757 --> 00:10:34.920
"rather than months."

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.900
The buyer in Akorn was able to demonstrate

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:39.330
that the magnitude of the change
in performance was material

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:42.030
and this poor performance
lasted for over a year.

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:44.460
This precedent probably isn't
very helpful to Musk's claim

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:45.930
that spam accounts mean that Twitter

00:10:45.930 --> 00:10:47.910
is dramatically overstating
its performance in ways

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:50.790
that could impact Twitter's
profitability down the line.

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:54.600
In fact, one could argue
that if there are more bots

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:55.710
than they know of,

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:58.710
then its profitability can
only go up even further

00:10:58.710 --> 00:10:59.850
'cause he'll get rid of the bots

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:02.700
and make it even more profitable per user.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:04.560
And this is especially
true since Musk said

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:07.380
he wanted to acquire Twitter
to improve the product

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:09.480
by eliminating the bots.

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:12.390
The SEC filings include voicemails by Musk

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:14.790
where he presses Twitter to
get the deal done quickly

00:11:14.790 --> 00:11:16.230
so he can fix the spam accounts

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:18.480
and make the company more profitable.

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:20.497
Musk then sent a voice
message saying, quote,

00:11:20.497 --> 00:11:21.487
"If the deal doesn't work,

00:11:21.487 --> 00:11:23.857
"given that I don't have
confidence in management

00:11:23.857 --> 00:11:26.047
"nor do I believe I can
drive the necessary change

00:11:26.047 --> 00:11:27.097
"in the public market,

00:11:27.097 --> 00:11:29.647
"I would need to reconsider
my position as a shareholder.

00:11:29.647 --> 00:11:32.107
"This is not a threat, it's
simply not a good investment

00:11:32.107 --> 00:11:34.710
"without the changes
that need to be made."

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:36.840
So even though Musk was at the time

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.430
the company's top shareholder,

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.050
he didn't think the stock
was a good investment

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:41.430
unless changes were made.

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:43.590
He then offered Twitter a share price

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:45.540
that was significantly above its value.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:48.030
In other words, Elon Musk
made it clear from the outset

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:49.890
that his interest in
Twitter had nothing to do

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:51.630
with its current profitability.

00:11:51.630 --> 00:11:54.120
And he knew that there were too many bots.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:56.310
He said he wanted to buy
Twitter to prove a point

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:57.240
about free speech,

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:59.130
and he said he wanted
to make a better product

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:01.020
by authenticating users, eliminating spam,

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:02.940
and adding an edit button.

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:03.773
So to sum it up,

00:12:03.773 --> 00:12:05.340
it looks like Twitter has
a pretty good argument

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:07.950
that they gave Musk all the
information that he needed

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:09.450
and was entitled to.

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:13.320
And further, Twitter can
certainly argue that, number one,

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:15.750
the bot numbers aren't
wrong, but even if they were,

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:18.660
it's still not a material adverse effect.

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:21.930
If the bot numbers are 7% instead of five,

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:23.880
that's probably not material.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:25.350
Though it's also probably
important to know

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:28.440
that one reason that Musk and
his lawyers have couched this

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:30.330
as a failure to provide info

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:33.960
instead of focusing on
claims of misrepresentation

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:35.850
is that the misrepresentations are covered

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:38.430
by the material adverse effect standard,

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:40.050
whereas the obligations of the company

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:43.470
found in the covenants are
generally considered material.

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:46.110
So if they breached their
obligations on the covenants,

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:49.980
they have a better chance of
getting out of the deal itself.

00:12:49.980 --> 00:12:51.960
Additionally, Musk's lawyers also claim

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:53.430
that Twitter failed to run their business

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:54.870
in the ordinary course of business

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:57.750
by firing two key
employees and losing others

00:12:57.750 --> 00:13:00.900
in violation of section 6.1
of the merger agreement.

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:02.610
That section of the
contract requires Twitter

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:04.860
to seek and obtain
consent before deviating

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:06.930
from its obligations
to conduct its business

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:10.140
in the ordinary course and
preserve substantially intact

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:11.490
the material components

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:13.680
of its current business organization.

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:15.750
And this is a very common term you see

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:17.550
in these kinds of agreements.

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:18.420
The reason is obvious.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:19.380
When you're buying a company

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:22.200
you want to make sure that,
that company continues

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:24.390
in more or less the same
vein than it started

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:27.360
and it doesn't completely
change their business

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:29.250
while you're in the process of buying it.

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:31.740
And here Twitter fired
its revenue product lead

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:34.163
and the general manager of consumer

00:13:34.163 --> 00:13:36.720
and other employees to it,

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:39.900
possibly because Elon Musk
was buying the company.

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:42.750
So Musk said that he
objected to Twitter firing

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:44.250
many talent acquisition employees

00:13:44.250 --> 00:13:46.140
and that he doesn't like
Twitter's hiring freeze.

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:48.967
Quote, "The company has not
received parent's consent

00:13:48.967 --> 00:13:51.037
"for changes in the
conduct of its business,

00:13:51.037 --> 00:13:54.120
"including for the specific
changes listed above."

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:56.370
The company's actions therefore
constitute a material breach

00:13:56.370 --> 00:13:58.710
of section 6.1 of the merger agreement.

00:13:58.710 --> 00:14:00.570
Now it is very rare
for deals to fall apart

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:02.190
over these kinds of obligations.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:06.360
Number one, Twitter legally
can't force anyone to stay,

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:08.820
not since the 13th Amendment was passed.

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:11.490
And additionally, sometimes as a company

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:13.170
you need to be able to terminate people.

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:16.020
That happens in the
regular course of business.

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:19.740
So we'll see if there's any hay
here, but it seems unlikely.

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:21.810
So let's talk about what's going to happen

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:24.210
in the ensuing litigation.

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:25.770
Nothing is certain in litigation,

00:14:25.770 --> 00:14:28.080
but based on the facts of this case

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:29.490
in terms of the legal arguments

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:31.320
I'd much rather be Twitter than Elon

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:32.970
in this particular situation.

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:34.980
Now normally when you're talking
about a breach of contract

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:36.390
and contract damages,

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:38.070
you're talking about expectation damages.

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:40.980
The party that didn't
breach is entitled to be

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:42.780
where they expected to be

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:44.550
after the contract was consummated,

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:46.650
and you measure that by
taking, in this case,

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:47.850
the promised price,

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:50.190
subtracting the current
price of the company,

00:14:50.190 --> 00:14:53.430
and the delta is the amount
that would be necessary

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:56.070
to compensate you for
where you expected to be.

00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:57.750
But here the parties negotiated

00:14:57.750 --> 00:15:00.540
a bilateral $1 billion
termination penalty.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:02.910
They considered that Twitter
might get a better offer

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:04.830
and might want to cancel this deal

00:15:04.830 --> 00:15:06.960
so they could go with the better offer.

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:09.330
And they also considered that
Musk might just, you know,

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:11.070
Musk everything up.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:13.350
But here this termination
provision is functioning

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:14.850
like a liquidated damages provision.

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:18.780
It sets the damages at $1 billion.

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:21.360
Now I suspect that if Elon
Musk could get out of this deal

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:23.190
by just paying 1$
billion and walking away,

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:26.460
he would probably take that
deal and just walk away.

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:29.070
But here's where things
get really, really sticky

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:32.370
because section 9.9B
of the merger agreement

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:34.110
gives Twitter the right to sue Musk

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:36.000
for specific performance.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:37.170
Now that is a legal term of art,

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:39.330
but that basically means
that Twitter can sue Musk

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:41.580
and it can seek an
injunction from the court

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:44.280
forcing Musk to close the deal.

00:15:44.280 --> 00:15:46.230
Now that is particularly complicated here

00:15:46.230 --> 00:15:48.060
because we are talking about a deal

00:15:48.060 --> 00:15:50.670
worth tens of billions of dollars.

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:52.800
And it's also complicated
because the deal requires

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:55.230
outside financing from several banks.

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:57.450
But the way that those
agreements are written,

00:15:57.450 --> 00:16:00.270
the banks don't have a
whole lot of outs here.

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:01.710
Now in law school they teach students

00:16:01.710 --> 00:16:04.410
that specific performance
is very rare, and it is.

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:06.570
Courts generally aren't in
the habit of forcing you

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:08.280
to do something that you don't want.

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:10.320
See, for example, the 13th Amendment.

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:12.480
But it most often happens with real estate

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:14.430
because it's thought
that, at least legally,

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:17.520
each piece of real
estate is a unique thing

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:20.490
that can't be compensated purely by money.

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:22.320
But when you're dealing with contracts,

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:24.600
courts would much rather
make people pay money

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:26.340
and go their separate ways.

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:28.350
However, it happens more frequently

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:30.870
in a context of mergers and acquisitions.

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:32.670
Delaware Courts have been known

00:16:32.670 --> 00:16:34.200
to order specific performance

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.960
in just such a situation as this.

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:38.520
Although it's more common for buyers

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.530
to try and force specific performance,

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:43.800
Delaware Courts agree that
sellers in the M&amp;A context

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:48.150
also have the right to force
the sale to completion.

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:50.790
So this is what happened
when a company called IBP,

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:52.380
the nation's top beef distributor,

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:54.480
entered into a merger
agreement with Tyson Foods,

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:56.490
the nation's leading chicken distributor.

00:16:56.490 --> 00:16:57.960
Both companies performed poorly

00:16:57.960 --> 00:16:59.910
after the merger agreement was announced

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:02.880
and Tyson's had buyer's remorse.

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:04.350
It tried to terminate the agreement,

00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:06.630
but IBP sued for specific performance.

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:09.090
And in addressing whether a
seller has the right to sue

00:17:09.090 --> 00:17:11.527
for specific performance,
the court found no, quote,

00:17:11.527 --> 00:17:14.107
"Compelling reason why sellers in mergers

00:17:14.107 --> 00:17:16.747
"and acquisitions transactions
should have less of a right

00:17:16.747 --> 00:17:19.110
"to demand specific
performance than buyers."

00:17:19.110 --> 00:17:21.690
So the court concluded that
Tyson had to go through

00:17:21.690 --> 00:17:22.770
with the purchase.

00:17:22.770 --> 00:17:25.170
The court said that even
figuring out the right measure

00:17:25.170 --> 00:17:27.360
of cash damages would be too hard

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:30.330
since the amount was presumably huge.

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:32.280
The court said that specific
performance was doable

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:33.990
since during trial Tyson's admitted

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:35.310
that it just wanted a lower price

00:17:35.310 --> 00:17:37.290
and that the acquisition
was still a good strategy

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:38.160
for the company.

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:39.690
The court also said that the shareholders

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:41.490
would be better served on the whole

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:43.170
by a specific performance remedy

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:44.820
rather than a large damages award

00:17:44.820 --> 00:17:48.090
that did nothing but cost
Tyson a large amount of money.

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:49.680
And now I need to explain some trivia

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:51.150
about the Delaware Courts of Chancery

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:53.460
where this case is going to end up.

00:17:53.460 --> 00:17:55.500
If you've been paying
attention to litigation,

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:56.940
you often hear people say

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:58.740
it'll take years to resolve anything,

00:17:58.740 --> 00:18:00.420
and that's usually the case.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:03.660
That is not the case in the
Delaware Chancery Courts.

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:05.670
The Delaware Chancery Courts are basically

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:07.560
business courts on steroids.

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:11.070
They handle mergers and
acquisitions for breakfast,

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:12.900
and Delaware made a name for itself

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:16.800
by being the most business-friendly
state in the country.

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:20.580
And the Delaware Chancery
Courts have a specific expertise

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:23.970
in dealing with these
enormous business disputes.

00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:25.500
And they are fast.

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.530
This could end up being resolved in months

00:18:28.530 --> 00:18:29.700
rather than years.

00:18:29.700 --> 00:18:32.880
And while the numbers are very high,

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:34.710
the dollar amounts are very high,

00:18:34.710 --> 00:18:37.590
this is not a particularly complex case.

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:41.010
In fact, Elon Musk is incredibly online,

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:45.300
so, so many of these statements
that he has made are public

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:47.940
and will absolutely be used against him.

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:49.200
So we'll probably have an answer

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:50.550
to this question pretty quickly.

00:18:50.550 --> 00:18:54.277
And at base, the litigation
outcomes are pretty stark:

00:18:54.277 --> 00:18:58.920
$1 billion in damages or
force Musk to pay $44 billion

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:01.410
to acquire a company he doesn't want.

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:04.380
Now in reality, depending
on how litigation goes,

00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:07.020
I'm sure all of the parties
will probably negotiate

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:08.550
something in between.

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:11.370
Maybe a bigger fee that
lets Elon walk away

00:19:11.370 --> 00:19:13.860
or maybe a lower price and
he consummates the deal.

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:16.470
It's entirely possible that
everything was just posturing

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:19.140
for him to get a better
deal in the long term.

00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:22.020
Or maybe Elon never wanted
Twitter in the first place.

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:22.980
Who knows?

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:25.020
But the important thing is that lawyers

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:26.820
are going to get paid.

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:28.710
And speaking of which,
there is the breaking news

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:31.170
that Twitter has indeed sued Elon Musk

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:33.780
for one count of breach of contract.

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:35.490
The company asked the
Delaware Court of Chancery

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:37.710
to enjoin Musk from further
breaches of contract,

00:19:37.710 --> 00:19:40.740
to compel him to fulfill
his legal obligations,

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:43.080
and to compel consummation of the merger

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:46.410
upon satisfaction of a few of
the outstanding conditions.

00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:50.070
Now maybe it's just me, but I
find this particular complaint

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:52.350
to be a pretty hilarious document.

00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:55.530
You can effectively put whatever
you want into a complaint.

00:19:55.530 --> 00:19:58.140
It's your opportunity to
tell your side of the story,

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:00.090
and Twitter is doing just that,

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:04.920
but it also just savages Elon
Musk using his own statements

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:06.870
and fills in quite a bit of the story.

00:20:06.870 --> 00:20:08.820
And it covers exactly
what you would expect,

00:20:08.820 --> 00:20:11.760
that Musk is in material
breach of the merger agreement.

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:15.210
Twitter alleges that Musk
didn't uphold his obligations,

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:17.880
including financing, public
disparagement of the company,

00:20:17.880 --> 00:20:20.130
and misuses information
in the whole, you know,

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:22.020
backing out of the deal thing.

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:24.150
But it also includes interesting tidbits

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:26.550
like Musk didn't even read
the information and reports

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:28.500
that Twitter gave him, even
though he's complaining

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:30.720
about not having enough information.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:33.150
And Twitter alleges that
he can't avail himself

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:34.860
of the $1 billion termination fee

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:36.750
and must specifically perform.

00:20:36.750 --> 00:20:41.130
He must consummate the
deal for $44 billion.

00:20:41.130 --> 00:20:42.720
But given that this is Elon Musk,

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:45.030
this is only the beginning
of the craziness.

